= Appendix A — Rapid Recall

Cover the answers after the dash and retrieve them before checking.

+ Health dimensions — physical, mental and social wellbeing.
+ A risk factor — an influence associated with a change in probability.
+ Handwashing before food — soap and clean running water where available.
+ When soap and water are unavailable — sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol.
+ A nutrient providing amino acids — protein.
+ A nutrient group providing energy — carbohydrate or fat.
+ Iron's important role — haemoglobin formation.
+ Iodine's important role — thyroid hormone production.
+ A way to limit cross-contamination — separate raw and ready-to-eat foods.
+ Antibiotics act against — susceptible bacteria, not viruses.
+ Tobacco smoke around others — second-hand exposure.
+ Alcohol's possible effect on driving — impaired judgement and reaction time.
+ Smoke in a room — a possible air-pollution exposure.
+ Clear-looking water — not necessarily safe.
+ Boiling — kills many organisms but does not remove all chemical pollutants.
+ Sanitation — safe management of human excreta.
+ A vector — an organism that can transmit an infectious agent.
+ Cause of malaria — Plasmodium parasites.
+ Common malaria vector — infected female Anopheles mosquito.
+ TB commonly affects — the lungs and can spread through the air.
+ Ordinary hugging and sharing food — do not spread HIV.
+ ART — treats HIV and can suppress the virus, but is not a cure.
+ Sustained undetectable HIV viral load — prevents sexual transmission according to WHO.
+ Vaccine — trains the immune system against particular infectious agents.
+ NCD examples — cardiovascular disease, cancer, diabetes and chronic respiratory disease.
+ Risk factor — changes probability, not certainty.
+ Persistent distress — may warrant qualified assessment and support.
+ Sudden severe chest pain or one-sided weakness — may require urgent help.
+ Before approaching an emergency — check scene safety.
+ Severe external bleeding — call for help and apply direct pressure when appropriate.
+ Embedded object — leave it in place and apply pressure around it.
+ Thermal burn — cool with cool running water when safe and feasible.
+ Suspected fracture — keep still and seek appropriate help.
+ Consent — voluntary, specific and capable of being withdrawn.
+ Pregnancy prevention and STI protection — related but distinct questions.
+ Condoms — reduce the risk of HIV and many STIs when used correctly and consistently.
+ STI status — cannot be established from appearance alone.
